    body: '다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When people set a goal, they often choose something like “I’ll do my best” or “I’ll study harder this semester.” Such goals sound reasonable, but they rarely lead to much change. The problem is that they give no clear target. Because “my best” can mean almost anything, it is easy to feel satisfied with whatever we actually do. Decades of research on goal setting have found that people usually perform better when their goals are specific and challenging than when they are simply told to do their best. A student who decides to solve twenty math problems every evening knows exactly what success looks like and can tell, at the end of each day, whether she has reached it. That clarity makes it easier to plan, to notice when she is falling behind, and to adjust her effort. If you want a goal to change your behavior, make it concrete enough that you can tell whether you have reached it.</div>',
