    body: '다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Most of us have heard an apology that made things worse. “I’m sorry if you were upset,” someone says, or “I’m sorry, but I was very busy that week.” Such words have the form of an apology, yet they quietly shift the focus away from what the speaker did. The first suggests that the problem lies in the other person’s feelings; the second turns the apology into an excuse. An apology that truly repairs a relationship does something different. It names the specific action that caused harm, admits responsibility for it without adding “but,” and shows that the speaker understands how it affected the other person. It may also include an offer to make things right. Such an apology can feel harder to give, because it leaves no room to protect our pride. That, however, is exactly why it works: the listener can see that we have honestly faced what we did.</div>',
