    slot: '주제',
    body: '다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Today salt is so cheap that we rarely think about it, but for most of human history it was a precious resource. Before refrigerators existed, keeping food from spoiling was one of the greatest challenges people faced. Meat and fish rot quickly because bacteria grow on them, and bacteria need water to survive. Salt draws water out of food and out of the bacteria themselves, making it very difficult for them to grow. By packing fish in salt or soaking meat in salty water, people could store food for months and carry it on long journeys. This allowed sailors to cross oceans, armies to march far from home, and communities to survive long winters. Because salt was so useful, it was widely traded and often taxed, and towns near salt deposits frequently grew wealthy. A simple white mineral thus played a quiet but powerful role in shaping how people lived and traveled.</div>',
