    body: '다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Why would a company spend millions on a television advertisement that says almost nothing about its product? The ad lists no prices and makes no claims about quality; it simply shows attractive people enjoying themselves. Economists have suggested that the content of such ads may matter less than their expense. A firm that pours money into advertising is betting that customers who try the product will return, because only repeat purchases can recover such a large investment. A firm selling a poor product, which customers would buy once and never again, cannot afford to make the same bet. Consumers therefore have reason to treat lavish advertising as a hint of quality, even if they never think about it in these terms. Seen in this light, the message of the ad is carried not by what it says but by ________. The more obviously wasteful the spending appears, the more convincingly it communicates the firm’s confidence.</div>',
