    body: '다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Collectors have long prized artists’ sketches, sometimes above the finished paintings for which they were made, and many viewers report returning to a favorite sketch again and again, finding something new each time. At first this seems odd, since a sketch is by definition incomplete: lines stop abruptly, shapes are only suggested, and whole areas of the page are left empty. Yet these very gaps may be the source of the sketch’s appeal. A finished painting presents its world fully resolved, leaving the viewer little to do except admire it. A sketch, by contrast, requires the viewer to complete what the artist merely implied — to see a shoulder in a single curve or a crowd in a handful of marks. In doing so, viewers become partners in the creation of the image, and the satisfaction they feel is partly the satisfaction of their own imaginative work. The power of a sketch, then, lies in ________.</div>',
