    level: 4,
    slot: '글의 순서 · 고난도',
    body: '주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">In negotiations, many people prefer to wait for the other side to name a price first, believing that revealing their own number puts them at a disadvantage.</div><div class="passage">(A) This happens because the opening offer shapes how the other side searches for information. A buyer who hears a high price begins to think of reasons the item might be worth that much, rather than reasons it might not. Each reason the buyer comes up with makes the high price seem a little less outrageous.<br>(B) Such a search does not lead the buyer to accept the anchor entirely, but it does stop the counteroffer from moving far from it. As a result, the party that speaks first frequently ends up with the better deal.<br>(C) Research on anchoring, however, suggests that this caution is often misplaced. The first number mentioned tends to pull the final agreement toward itself, even when both parties know it was chosen strategically.</div>',
    type: 'choice',
    choices: ['(A) − (C) − (B)', '(B) − (A) − (C)', '(B) − (C) − (A)', '(C) − (A) − (B)', '(C) − (B) − (A)'],
    answer: 4,
    concepts: ['e-order'],
