    body: '주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Expert chess players can glance at a board from a real game for a few seconds and then reproduce the positions of almost all the pieces. The feat seems all the more impressive because the board may hold more than twenty pieces scattered across sixty-four squares.</div><div class="passage">(A) That conclusion, however, collapses when the pieces are placed randomly rather than taken from actual games. Under these conditions, experts perform barely better than beginners.<br>(B) Novices, given the same brief look, typically recall only a handful. It would be natural to conclude that experts simply possess superior memories.<br>(C) Their advantage, it seems, lies not in memory itself but in knowledge: years of play allow them to see meaningful groups of pieces where a novice sees many separate ones. A master looking at a familiar attack formation recalls it as a single unit, much as a reader remembers a word rather than its individual letters. Random boards contain no such groups to recognize.</div>',
