    body: '주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When photography appeared in the nineteenth century, many painters feared that their profession was finished.</div><div class="passage">(A) Some turned to the fleeting effects of light, others to bold, unnatural color, and still others to the inner world of emotion. Movements such as Impressionism grew partly out of this freedom. In this sense, the new technology did not end painting so much as push it in new directions.<br>(B) Yet the loss of this task turned out to be a kind of liberation. No longer obliged to compete with the machine at accuracy, painters were free to explore what the camera could not capture.<br>(C) Their fear was understandable. For centuries, one of painting’s chief tasks had been to record faces, places, and events accurately, and a camera could now do this faster and more cheaply than any artist. Portrait painters in particular watched as customers who once sat for hours in their studios now visited photographers instead.</div>',
