    body: '주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Before the nineteenth century, each town set its clocks by the sun, so that noon in one city came a few minutes earlier or later than noon in a neighboring one.</div><div class="passage">(A) For most of history, such small differences caused no trouble. A traveler moving at the speed of a horse could hardly notice that his watch had drifted out of step with local time.<br>(B) To solve the problem, railway companies adopted a single standard time across their networks, and governments later followed. Our sense that the hour is the same everywhere in a region is thus a fairly recent invention.<br>(C) The railways changed this. Trains crossed many towns in a single day, and a timetable that listed dozens of different local times was confusing at best and dangerous at worst, since trains shared the same tracks. A train leaving one town at noon could arrive at the next at a time that, by the local clocks, seemed impossible.</div>',
