    body: '다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">We tend to picture scientific instruments as servants of curiosity: a scientist asks a question, then builds or borrows a tool to answer it. The history of science often runs the other way. Before the microscope, no one could have asked what a drop of pond water contained, because there was no reason to suspect it contained anything at all. The tiny moving creatures that early microscopists described came as a complete surprise, and a whole field of study grew up around them. Before the telescope, questions about the mountains of the Moon or the moons of Jupiter had no meaning; they became pressing only after an instrument revealed that there was something to ask about. Even the form of the questions was set by the tools: researchers asked what could be magnified, measured, or counted, because those were the operations their instruments performed. Seen this way, instruments do not simply extend the reach of a curiosity that already exists. They ________.</div>',
