    body: '[1~2] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.<div class="passage">When a city’s roads become congested, the most obvious remedy is to build more of them. A new lane is added, and for a few months traffic flows smoothly. Then, often within a few years, the jams return, sometimes worse than before. To many drivers this seems like bad luck or poor planning, but it follows from a simple feature of how people <u>(a) respond</u> to cost.<br>Time is the main price of driving. When a road is crowded, a trip that should take twenty minutes takes an hour, and many people decide that the trip is not worth it. They travel at another time, take a train, shop closer to home, or skip the journey altogether. Widening the road <u>(b) raises</u> this price. Suddenly the trip is quick again, and the people who had given it up begin to return. Others, encouraged by the shorter commute, move to homes farther from the city. Each new driver adds only a little to the traffic, but together they <u>(c) fill</u> the new space until the road is as slow as before.<br>None of this means that building roads is pointless. A wider road may carry more people than the old one did, even if each of them still moves slowly. But it does mean that adding capacity cannot, by itself, <u>(d) guarantee</u> lasting relief from congestion, because the demand it is meant to satisfy is not fixed; it <u>(e) grows</u> in response to the supply. Planners who understand this tend to think less about how to move more cars and more about how to give people good reasons to travel differently — through reliable public transport, nearby services, or charges that reflect the true cost of driving at the busiest hours.</div>밑줄 친 (a)~(e) 중에서 문맥상 낱말의 쓰임이 적절하지 <b>않은</b> 것은?',
