    level: 5,
    slot: '빈칸 추론 · 고난도',
    body: '다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Most of us know that an apology should accept responsibility, but we often weaken our apologies without noticing. The usual culprit is the explanation. When we have hurt someone, we want them to understand that we did not mean to, that we were exhausted, or that the circumstances were unusual, and so we offer our reasons along with our regret. The reasons may all be true. Yet the injured person tends to hear them differently from the way we intend: each explanation, however accurate, shifts attention from the harm they suffered to the difficulties we faced, and implies that the harm was, in a sense, nobody’s fault. Research on reconciliation suggests that explanations are not useless, but that their effect depends heavily on timing. Offered after the injured party feels that the hurt has been fully acknowledged, an account of what happened can help to rebuild trust. Offered before, it ________.</div>',
