    body: '다음 글에서 전체 흐름과 관계 <b>없는</b> 문장은?<div class="passage">When companies tear down walls to create open-plan offices, the usual justification is that employees who can see one another will talk more and collaborate more freely. ① Studies that have tracked workers before and after such moves, however, often find the opposite: face-to-face conversations decline, sometimes sharply, while emails and messages increase. ② The reason seems to be that, surrounded by colleagues who can overhear everything, people seek privacy in the only ways left to them, putting on headphones, avoiding eye contact, and moving conversations to screens. ③ Open offices are also cheaper to build and maintain, since they require less floor space per worker and fewer separate heating and lighting systems. ④ In effect, removing the physical barriers leads workers to put up social ones. ⑤ The lesson is not that walls are always bad or always good, but that the amount of interaction in a space depends less on what people can see than on how much control they feel they have over being seen and heard.</div>',
